.TH SINDOOK-VERIFY 1 "2026-08-18" "sindook 0.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sindook-verify \- confirm sealed files decrypt cleanly, writing nothing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sindook verify
(\fB\-i\fP \fIIDENTITY\fP | \fB\-p\fP | \fB\-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP)
[\fB\-identity-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-glob\fP \fIPATTERN\fP]...
[\fIFILE\fP...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Fully decrypts and authenticates sealed files without writing
plaintext anywhere. Confirms a backup will actually open before it is
needed. Every file is checked even if an earlier one fails; the exit
code is non-zero if any did.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fP \fIIDENTITY\fP
identity file (prompts if passphrase-protected); use \fB@default\fP
for the identity selected by
.BR sindook-init (1)
.TP
\fB\-p\fP
verify with a passphrase, prompted at the terminal
.TP
\fB\-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP
read the passphrase from \fIFILE\fP instead
.TP
\fB\-identity-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP
read a protected identity's passphrase from \fIFILE\fP
.TP
\fB\-glob\fP \fIPATTERN\fP
add files matched by a portable filesystem pattern, repeatable
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sindook verify -i my.key backups/*.sindook
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sindook (1),
.BR sindook-open (1),
.BR sindook-inspect (1)
